WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:09.250
Let's talk about IPLs because it's more than just flashing up a welcome to ZOS splash screen

2
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.250
and then suddenly you're ready to run enterprise workloads.

3
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:18.250
An initial program load is technically the act of loading a copy of the operating system,

4
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:25.250
typically from DASD into the processor's main storage area and running it or executing it.

5
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:31.250
That disk that you point an L part to for it to IPL off of has what's called a bootstrap module

6
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.250
at cylinder zero track zero.

7
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:40.250
What that means is literally the first bit of data the OS sees when it reads off of it.

8
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.250
The bootstrap module is loaded into real memory at address zero.

9
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:50.250
That's the first available memory slot that the L part has access to and then control is passed to it.

10
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.250
The bootstrap isn't the main operating system.

11
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:59.250
It's just enough to assemble the pieces and information needed to bring up the full operating system.

12
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:03.250
So the bootstrap reads the IPL control program.

13
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:10.250
That's the IEA IPL zero zero, also known as the IPL text and passes control to that.

14
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.250
More on that in a bit.

15
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:18.250
This in turn starts the more complex tasks of loading the full operating system and bringing that up.

16
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:24.250
On its way up the OS looks for what's called a load parameter, which consists of eight characters.

17
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.250
And this is a nice and compact way of telling the L part how we want it to IPL.

18
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:35.250
The first four characters are the device number of the IODF device.

19
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.250
That's what contains all the hardware and software configuration for the system.

20
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:45.250
Characters five and six contain the load member more on that in a bit as well.

21
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:51.250
The seventh position indicates what messages and prompts should be displayed at IPL time.

22
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:57.250
And in the last position way at the end is the IEA and you see zero member.

23
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.250
Usually pronounced IEA new zero.

24
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.250
Guess what? More on that in a bit too.

25
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:08.250
During IPL the system will begin looking for the information it needs to fully come up.

26
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.250
First thing to look for is the load xx member.

27
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.250
This tells us some vital information about the system.

28
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:28.250
So the IPL resource initialization module, IRIM, first attempts to locate the load xx member in the cis0.IPL param data set.

29
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.250
This is just a data set where the load xx member is kept.

30
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.250
It's actually not labeled as load xx.

31
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:44.250
That's just the common way of saying it could be load zero zero or load zero one or whatever we told it look for in characters five and six of the load parameter.

32
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:54.250
If it's not there it'll look in cis1.IPL param and then in cis2.IPL param and all the way up to cis9.IPL param.

33
00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:00.250
If it cannot find the load xx member the system will enter a non restartable weight state.

34
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:06.250
The IPL was not successful and trying it again won't help until we fix something.

35
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:13.250
If the cis dot whatever IPL param couldn't be found on the IODF volume the search continues.

36
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.250
And it will check in the cis1.parmli data set of the IODF volume.

37
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:22.250
By the way of volume is just a logical dasd disk.

38
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.250
You can imagine it like a disk drive here.

39
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:32.250
Anyway, if it can't find it there it'll look in cis1.parmli on another volume called the cis res volume.

40
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.250
And if I can't find it there then we're done.

41
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.250
The IPL was unsuccessful.

42
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:46.250
For a full breakdown of that search process at IPL time there is a great document included in the notes for this lesson.

43
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:52.250
But the short version of this is that the IPL system needs to find its load xx member.

44
00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:59.250
And once it finds it it'll be opened and then it will have all the information it needs including the nucleus suffix,

45
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:05.250
the master catalog name and the suffix of the IEA cis xx members.

46
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:10.250
Now we mentioned a couple of things in there without going into full detail.

47
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:15.250
Once you get a hands-on system and you start looking at those members and checking out those screens

48
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:20.250
you'll get a better picture for what type of data is in them and how they all fit together.

49
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:24.250
These are all just a couple of ways that systems can start up.

50
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:32.250
And as you can see it's important that all the pieces be set up and in the right place when we want to bring up or IPL and outpar.
